<div style="text-align:center; padding: 40px 0; border-bottom: 3px solid #e67e22; margin-bottom: 40px;">
  <div style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: bold; color: #e67e22; letter-spacing: 2px; text-transform: uppercase;">Final Presentation Content Deck</div>
  <h1 style="border:none; font-size: 30px; margin-top: 10px; color: #1a1a2e;">Kolam Inverse: Symbolic Image-to-Equation Reconstruction with Topological and Symmetry Invariance</h1>
  <p style="font-size: 16px; color: #555; max-width: 750px; margin: 10px auto;">From Discrete Pixel Rasters to Continuous Closed-Form Geometric Primitives via Discriminative Edge Inference, Dihedral Symmetry, and Grammar Induction</p>
  <div style="margin-top: 20px; font-size: 14px; color: #7f8c8d;">
    <strong>Author:</strong> Neelanjana J. Anil &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Domain:</strong> Computer Vision, Ethnomathematics & Symbolic AI
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SLIDE 1 -->
<div class="slide-container">
  <div class="slide-num">Slide 01</div>
  <h1>Introduction & Cultural-Computational Context</h1>
  <h2>Bridging Traditional Ethnomathematics and Modern Geometric Inverse Graphics</h2>
  
  <h3>Slide Bullets</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The Tradition of Kolam:</strong> An ancient South Indian ritual drawing created with rice flour on doorsteps around rectilinear or hexagonal dot matrices (<em>pulli</em>).</li>
    <li><strong>The Computational Nature of Kolams:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>They are not unconstrained sketches; they represent <strong>continuous, non-intersecting Eulerian loops</strong>.</li>
        <li>They exhibit rigid planar graph embeddings, fractal scaling, and discrete dihedral group symmetries ($D_4$ and Klein-4 $V_4$).</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>The Inverse Problem:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li><em>Forward generation</em> is easy: apply a formal grammar or turtle-graphics sequence.</li>
        <li><em>Inverse reconstruction</em> is difficult: given only noisy image pixels, automatically infer the underlying symbolic equations (lines, arcs, Béziers) and grammatical rules.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="speaker-notes">
    <div class="speaker-notes-title">🎤 Speaker Notes</div>
    <p>"Good morning. Today, I am presenting our work on Kolam Inverse. For centuries, Kolams have been practiced in South India as an ephemeral art form. To a computer scientist, however, a Kolam represents a rare convergence of discrete graph theory, continuous differential geometry, and formal language theory. Our objective is to solve the inverse problem: transforming raw pixel photographs into exact, continuous mathematical equations while strictly preserving topological continuity and group symmetry."</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SLIDE 2 -->
<div class="slide-container">
  <div class="slide-num">Slide 02</div>
  <h1>Problem Statement & Motivation</h1>
  <h2>The Fundamental Failure of Autoregressive Neural Decoders in Geometric Inverse Graphics</h2>
  
  <h3>Slide Bullets</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The Discrete-to-Continuous Semantic Gap:</strong> Pixels are quantized on a sensor grid, while mathematical paths exist in continuous real coordinate space $\mathbb{R}^2$.</li>
    <li><strong>The Catastrophic Drift of Autoregressive Models:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>Sequence decoders (e.g., SketchRNN, autoregressive Transformers) predicting sequential coordinates $(x_t, y_t)$ suffer from <strong>compounding positional drift</strong> across long horizons ($700+$ steps).</li>
        <li>Result: By step 200, cumulative errors cause the trajectory to miss closing vertices, creating open dangling loops and spurious jump strokes.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>The Topological Blindness of Pixel Loss Functions:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>Standard losses (MSE, L1, Chamfer) only reward spatial closeness; they are mathematically blind to whether loops actually enclose their respective dot anchors.</li>
        <li>Graph-theoretic invariants (even degree $d(v) \equiv 0 \pmod 2$, loop count $eta_1$) are completely ignored.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="speaker-notes">
    <div class="speaker-notes-title">🎤 Speaker Notes</div>
    <p>"Why does standard deep learning fail at this task? If you train a Transformer or an LSTM to output coordinate points sequentially, an error of just one pixel at step 30 snowballs by step 700. The loop fails to close, producing an invalid open curve. Furthermore, standard pixel-based loss functions are topologically blind: they do not penalize a model for breaking loop connectivity or missing dot anchors."</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SLIDE 3 -->
<div class="slide-container">
  <div class="slide-num">Slide 03</div>
  <h1>Literature Review & Gap Analysis</h1>
  <h2>From Classical Formal Grammars to Deep Vector Graphics</h2>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th style="width: 25%;">Domain</th>
      <th style="width: 45%;">Prior Work & Approaches</th>
      <th style="width: 30%;">Critical Gaps / Limitations</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Ethnomathematics & Formal Grammars</strong></td>
      <td>Siromoney et al. (1974) array grammars; Marcia Ascher (2002) ethnomathematics of sand drawings; Robinson (2007) knot theory of Kolams.</td>
      <td>Strictly forward-generative paper models. No automated vision system to infer grammars from raw images.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Autoregressive Sketch Synthesis</strong></td>
      <td>SketchRNN (Ha & Eck, 2017), PolyGen (Nash et al., 2020), Co-Stroke (2022).</td>
      <td>Severe compounding error over long steps ($>200$). Zero guarantee of closed Eulerian circuits.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Differentiable Vector Graphics</strong></td>
      <td>DiffVG (Li et al., 2020), LIVE (Ma et al., 2022), VectorFusion (2023).</td>
      <td>Continuous path rasterization optimization. Trapped in local minima; cannot discover discrete grammars or symmetry groups.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Our Technical Advantage:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Topological guarantee: Deterministic traversal on candidate lattice edges completely eliminates autoregressive drift.</li>
    <li>Symbolic output: Produces closed-form parametric and algebraic equations with bounded domains.</li>
    <li>Group theory & grammar: Recovers Klein-4 ($V_4$) dihedral symmetry and hierarchical Re-Pair grammar rules.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="speaker-notes">
    <div class="speaker-notes-title">🎤 Speaker Notes</div>
    <p>"In the literature, research has been divided into two disconnected camps: formal linguists who wrote generative grammars on paper, and modern deep learning models that treat sketch curves as unconstrained bezier paths. Our work bridges this divide by using computer vision to extract candidate lattices, discrete mathematics to enforce Eulerian continuity, and continuous numerical optimization to fit parametric equations."</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SLIDE 4 -->
<div class="slide-container">
  <div class="slide-num">Slide 04</div>
  <h1>Project Objectives & Scientific Ground Rules</h1>
  <h2>Rigorous Formulation, Blind Protocols, and Verifiable Milestones</h2>

  <h3>Core Objectives:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Objective 1: End-to-End Blind Extraction:</strong> Build a pipeline processing raw input photos into continuous equations without human intervention.</li>
    <li><strong>Objective 2: Dual-Model Empirical Benchmark:</strong> Objectively compare a <em>Tuned Deterministic Lattice Baseline</em> against a <em>Deep Neural Edge Classifier (CNN+MLP)</em>.</li>
    <li><strong>Objective 3: Closed-Form Mathematical Fitting:</strong> Fit piecewise primitives (straight lines, circular arcs, cubic Béziers) using Orthogonal Distance Regression (ODR).</li>
    <li><strong>Objective 4: Symmetry Orbit Reduction:</strong> Prove and leverage Klein-4 ($V_4$) dihedral symmetry to achieve an exact $pprox 3	imes$ parameter reduction.</li>
    <li><strong>Objective 5: Hierarchical Grammar Induction:</strong> Discover structural motifs via recursive digram pairing (Re-Pair).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Scientific Ground Rules Enforced During Execution:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><span class="highlight-badge">Rule R1</span> <strong>Script-Generated Numbers Only:</strong> Zero hardcoded metrics. All figures come from logged execution.</li>
    <li><span class="highlight-badge">Rule R2</span> <strong>Blind Inference:</strong> Test CSV files renamed to <code>csv_BLIND_LOCKED</code>; models had zero coordinate leakage.</li>
    <li><span class="highlight-badge">Rule R3</span> <strong>Clean Carve-Out:</strong> 324 training, 36 validation, 40 held-out test designs.</li>
    <li><span class="highlight-badge">Rule R4</span> <strong>Negative & Identity Controls:</strong> Synthetic black controls, shifted controls, and identity swap tests enforced.</li>
    <li><span class="highlight-badge">Rule R5</span> <strong>Transparent Reporting:</strong> Negative results and model limitations reported honestly.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="speaker-notes">
    <div class="speaker-notes-title">🎤 Speaker Notes</div>
    <p>"Our project adhered strictly to empirical research protocols. When testing our models, all ground-truth coordinate files were physically locked and inaccessible to the pipeline. Every metric was generated automatically by reproducible evaluation scripts, and all negative controls—such as testing against blank images or mismatched designs—were verified."</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SLIDE 5 -->
<div class="slide-container">
  <div class="slide-num">Slide 05</div>
  <h1>System Workflow & Architecture</h1>
  <h2>The Six-Stage Pipeline: From Pixels to Symbolic Geometry</h2>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Stage</th>
      <th>Operation</th>
      <th>Algorithmic Mechanism</th>
      <th>Output Artifact</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Stage 01</strong></td>
      <td>Raw Input Capture</td>
      <td>Ingestion of calibrated pulli scans or blind uploads.</td>
      <td>RGB Image Matrix</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Stage 02</strong></td>
      <td>Cleaned Mask</td>
      <td>Otsu thresholding + Area Opening ($\le 16	ext{ px}$). <br><em>Verified: 0.0 px stroke skeleton erosion.</em></td>
      <td>Binary Stroke Mask</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Stage 03</strong></td>
      <td>Medial Skeleton</td>
      <td>Zhang-Suen morphological thinning algorithm.</td>
      <td>1-pixel Medial Axis</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Stage 04</strong></td>
      <td>Graph & Trajectory</td>
      <td>Junction detection ($N(p) \ge 3$) + Straight-through crossing rule ($\max \cos 	heta$).</td>
      <td>Ordered Edge Graph</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Stage 05</strong></td>
      <td>Equation Fitting</td>
      <td>Orthogonal Distance Regression (ODR) for Lines, Arcs, and Cubic Béziers.</td>
      <td>Piecewise Parametric & Algebraic Equations</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Stage 06</strong></td>
      <td>Verification Overlay</td>
      <td>Euclidean distance transform + Rasterization alignment.</td>
      <td>F1 / Chamfer Score & Overlay Map</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <div class="speaker-notes">
    <div class="speaker-notes-title">🎤 Speaker Notes</div>
    <p>"Here is our complete system architecture. Notice Stage 2: we use morphological opening to eliminate pulli dot anchors while rigorously confirming that exactly zero pixels of the stroke skeleton are eroded. In Stage 4, crossing junctions are traversed using the straight-through rule, maximizing cosine similarity between incoming and outgoing directions to prevent artificial kinks."</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SLIDE 6 -->
<div class="slide-container">
  <div class="slide-num">Slide 06</div>
  <h1>Mathematical Foundations</h1>
  <h2>Continuous Differential Primitives, Dihedral Groups, and Grammar Induction</h2>

  <h3>1. Closed-Form Primitive Formulations</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Straight Line Segment:</strong>
      <br>Parametric: $\mathbf{r}(t) = (1-t)\mathbf{p}_0 + t\mathbf{p}_1, \quad t \in [0, 1]$
      <br>Implicit Algebraic: $(y_1 - y_0)x - (x_1 - x_0)y + (x_1 y_0 - x_0 y_1) = 0, \quad x \in [x_{\min}, x_{\max}], y \in [y_{\min}, y_{\max}]$
    </li>
    <li><strong>Circular Arc:</strong>
      <br>Parametric: $\mathbf{r}(t) = [h + r\cos(	heta_0 + t\Delta	heta), \; k + r\sin(	heta_0 + t\Delta	heta)]^T, \quad t \in [0, 1]$
      <br>Implicit Algebraic: $(x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2 = r^2$
    </li>
    <li><strong>Cubic Bézier Curve:</strong>
      <br>Parametric: $\mathbf{B}(t) = (1-t)^3\mathbf{P}_0 + 3(1-t)^2 t\mathbf{P}_1 + 3(1-t)t^2\mathbf{P}_2 + t^3\mathbf{P}_3, \quad t \in [0, 1]$
      <br>Fitted via Levenberg-Marquardt non-linear least squares minimizing perpendicular residual distance.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Klein-4 ($V_4$) Dihedral Group Reduction</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Symmetry group $V_4 = \{ e, \sigma_x, \sigma_y, R_{180} \}$ acting on canvas coordinates with center $(c_x, c_y)$:
      $$\sigma_x(x, y) = (2c_x - x, y), \quad \sigma_y(x, y) = (x, 2c_y - y), \quad R_{180}(x, y) = (2c_x - x, 2c_y - y)$$
    </li>
    <li><strong>Exact Reduction:</strong> Storing only the canonical seed quadrant $\mathcal{Q}_1$ allows regeneration of all 4 quadrants, reducing required storage by up to <strong>2.94x</strong>.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="speaker-notes">
    <div class="speaker-notes-title">🎤 Speaker Notes</div>
    <p>"Every piece in our model is a rigorous mathematical object. Lines and arcs are provided with both parametric and algebraic implicit representations with bounded domains. For Béziers, we fit four control points via orthogonal distance regression. Using Klein-4 group theory, we mathematically prove that every dataset Kolam can be regenerated from its canonical seed quadrant, eliminating four-fold redundancy."</p>
  </div>
</div>

