  <h3>Empirical Findings on Lattice Geometry:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Zero Candidate Edge Ambiguity (0.00%):</strong> Across all 2,324 candidate lattice edges of kolam19, no edge midpoint lies within $0.1$ lattice units of any other edge. All candidate edges are disjoint and separable.</li>
    <li><strong>Symmetry Verification:</strong> 100% of the true edge sets across all three families were verified to be strictly invariant under $V_4$ quadrant reflection.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="speaker-notes">
    <div class="speaker-notes-title">🎤 Speaker Notes</div>
    <p>"We tested across three complete families from Kaggle's pulli dataset, scaling from a 19x19 grid up to a massive 109x109 lattice with over 23,000 moves. We proved mathematically that there is zero spatial ambiguity between candidate edges: the minimum separation between any two distinct candidate edges is 0.1118 lattice units, well above our 0.1 tolerance."</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SLIDE 8 -->
<div class="slide-container">
  <div class="slide-num">Slide 08</div>
  <h1>Learning Framework & Experimental Audit</h1>
  <h2>Deterministic Baseline vs. Deep Neural Edge Classifier</h2>

  <h3>1. Model Architectures Tested Under Strict Blind Conditions:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tuned Deterministic Baseline:</strong> Direct raster-skeleton correlation along candidate lattice paths. Hyperparameters tuned on validation: $	ext{tol} = 0.10, 	ext{thresh} = 0.90$.</li>
    <li><strong>Neural Edge Classifier (CNN + MLP):</strong> 3-layer Convolutional Feature Extractor + Spatial Coordinate Patch Sampler + 2-layer MLP classifier predicting edge existence $P(e \in E \mid I)$. Optimal threshold on validation: $	au = 0.60$.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Comparative Results on 40 Blind Held-Out Designs:</h3>
  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Model</th>
      <th>Edge Precision</th>
      <th>Edge Recall</th>
      <th>Edge $F_1$</th>
      <th>ROC-AUC</th>
      <th>Move Match Rate</th>
      <th>Correct Prefix</th>
      <th>Chamfer</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Tuned Baseline</strong></td>
      <td><strong>99.94%</strong></td>
      <td><strong>99.94%</strong></td>
      <td><strong>99.94%</strong></td>
      <td><strong>1.0000</strong></td>
      <td><strong>90.41%</strong></td>
      <td><strong>654.5 / 724</strong></td>
      <td><strong>0.131 lat</strong></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Neural Classifier</strong></td>
      <td>83.89%</td>
      <td>80.24%</td>
      <td>82.01%</td>
      <td>0.9613</td>
      <td>12.83%</td>
      <td>90.5 / 724</td>
      <td>2.643 lat</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>3. Key Research Insight:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The neural model achieved strong edge classification ($82.01\% F_1, 0.961 	ext{ AUC}$), but minor edge dropouts break vertex degree parity, causing sequential traversal to terminate earlier ($90.5$ vs $654.5$ moves).</li>
    <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> In strictly constrained geometric lattice domains, deterministic projection vastly outperforms unconstrained neural models.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="speaker-notes">
    <div class="speaker-notes-title">🎤 Speaker Notes</div>
    <p>"This slide presents a major finding of our research. The deep neural classifier reached an 82% F1 score and a 0.96 ROC-AUC under blind conditions. However, the deterministic baseline achieved 99.94% F1 and 90.4% move match. Why? In graph-constrained geometric lattices, exact coordinate correlation outperforms neural feature learning. Furthermore, even a 1% edge error in a neural model breaks Eulerian cycle continuity."</p>
  </div>
</div>

