  <h3>2. Generalization on Arbitrary Uploads (The Complex Mandala Test):</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Tested on a non-Kolam, arbitrary 556-piece mandala drawing:
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Fit Fidelity ($F_1$ vs Extracted Skeleton):</strong> <span class="highlight-badge">98.07%</span></li>
        <li><strong>Stroke Precision:</strong> 98.39% &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Stroke Recall:</strong> 97.75%</li>
        <li><strong>Chamfer Distance:</strong> <strong>0.52 pixels</strong> (sub-pixel alignment)</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Scientific Transparency on Topology (Betti Numbers $eta_0, eta_1$):</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Raster Betti number comparison showed an anomaly ($pprox 43\%$ loop preservation) because the thresholded image mask contained pulli dots, while piecewise equations lacked post-fitting node snapping.</li>
    <li><strong>Honest Action:</strong> Kept collapsed and explicitly marked <em>"under correction"</em> rather than presenting misleading claims.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="speaker-notes">
    <div class="speaker-notes-title">🎤 Speaker Notes</div>
    <p>"When our fitted equations are rasterized and compared against the original image skeletons, we reach 92% to 94% F1 within half a lattice unit, and over 99.6% within one unit. Even on an arbitrary blind mandala upload with 556 pieces, our engine achieved 98.07% fit fidelity and a sub-pixel Chamfer distance of 0.52 pixels. Furthermore, when our Betti number comparison showed an anomaly due to open piecewise nodes, we honestly flagged it as under correction."</p>
  </div>
</div>

