<!-- SLIDE 11 -->
<div class="slide-container">
  <div class="slide-num">Slide 11</div>
  <h1>Scope, Limitations & Critical Lessons</h1>
  <h2>Honest Boundary Conditions and Failure Modes</h2>

  <h3>1. The Superiority of Deterministic Algorithms in Structured Domains:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>In unconstrained domains (e.g., natural images), deep neural networks dominate.</li>
    <li>In structured, graph-constrained geometric lattices, <strong>deterministic algorithms with mathematical priors achieve 99.94% accuracy vs 82.01% for CNNs</strong>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Sensitivity of Planar Lattices to In-Plane Rotation:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The lattice edge classifier is robust to Gaussian noise ($\sigma=0.05, 79.56\% F_1$), blur ($1	ext{px}, 80.31\% F_1$), and JPEG compression ($80.30\% F_1$).</li>
    <li>However, it is highly sensitive to camera rotation ($2^\circ 	ext{ tilt}$ drops $F_1$ to $31.36\%$) because rotated vertices drift away from orthogonal sampling points.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Real-World Mobile Photos Require Calibration:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The current pipeline is validated on calibrated, orthogonal scans. Real-world mobile photos with perspective tilt or non-uniform outdoor lighting require 4-point homography calibration before edge inference.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="speaker-notes">
    <div class="speaker-notes-title">🎤 Speaker Notes</div>
    <p>"A critical lesson of our research is that deep learning is not always the best tool for every vision problem. When mathematical constraints are absolute, a hybrid architecture—using computer vision for feature extraction and discrete mathematics for traversal and fitting—guarantees topological validity. We also honestly acknowledge that real-world mobile photos with camera tilt will require perspective homography correction."</p>
  </div>
</div>

