  <h1>Conclusion & Future Horizons</h1>
  <h2>Summary of Contributions and Research Directions</h2>

  <h3>Summary of Contributions:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Demonstrated an end-to-end, blind inverse graphics pipeline translating discrete Kolam images into continuous closed-form equations.</li>
    <li>Achieved <strong>99.94% edge detection accuracy</strong> on 40 held-out designs and <strong>92%–94% image F1</strong> at half a lattice unit.</li>
    <li>Mathematically proved and verified an exact <strong>2.89x to 2.94x parameter reduction</strong> via Klein-4 ($V_4$) dihedral symmetry.</li>
    <li>Demonstrated up to <strong>4.93x grammar compression</strong> using Re-Pair hierarchical induction.</li>
    <li>Verified generalization on complex non-Kolam artwork (98.07% fit fidelity on a 556-primitive mandala).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Future Research Directions:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Mobile Perspective Rectification:</strong> Automated 4-point homography estimation using pulli dots as vanishing point references.</li>
    <li><strong>Constrained Spline Optimization:</strong> Global node-snapping to guarantee shared junction coordinates for 100% closed Betti loop counts.</li>
    <li><strong>Robotic Toolpath Synthesis:</strong> Direct G-code compilation from induced equations for automated CNC sand plotters and physical drawing robots.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="speaker-notes">
    <div class="speaker-notes-title">🎤 Speaker Notes</div>
    <p>"In conclusion, we have shown that traditional geometric art can be translated into exact, continuous mathematical equations with verifiable fidelity and symmetry invariance. This work preserves cultural heritage while advancing symbolic computer vision. Thank you, and I look forward to your questions."</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SLIDE 13 -->
<div class="slide-container">
  <div class="slide-num">Slide 13 (Backup)</div>
  <h1>Anticipated Faculty Q&A Defense Strategy</h1>
  <h2>Prepared Strategic Responses for Reviewers</h2>

  <div class="qna-box">
    <strong>Q1: "Why did your CNN only achieve 82% when modern vision models easily get 95%+?"</strong><br>
    <em>Defense:</em> "Standard vision benchmarks classify high-level semantic categories where global texture suffices. Here, edge classification requires sub-pixel coordinate alignment. When edges are separated by only 0.11 lattice units, small receptive field misalignments cause false positives. The deterministic detector achieved 99.94% because it projects directly along the known lattice geometry."
  </div>

  <div class="qna-box">
    <strong>Q2: "Why is the topology preservation box showing ~43% in the screenshots?"</strong><br>
    <em>Defense:</em> "The 43.7% Betti number preservation is an artifact of the raster comparison: the thresholded image mask contains pulli dots that create extra topological holes ($eta_1$), while our piecewise equations are fitted per branch and have sub-pixel gaps at junctions before node snap-rounding. We recognized this discrepancy and honestly marked it 'under correction' rather than presenting misleading claims."
  </div>

  <div class="qna-box">
    <strong>Q3: "Can your model handle a photo taken from an angle with a smartphone?"</strong><br>
    <em>Defense:</em> "Currently, the pipeline requires an orthogonal view. If taken at an angle, perspective foreshortening distorts regular lattice spacing. In future work, we plan to incorporate a 4-point homography estimation step using the pulli dots as vanishing point references to unwarp photos before feeding them to the equation engine."
  </div>

  <div class="qna-box">
    <strong>Q4: "Why did you choose Klein-4 symmetry over standard rotation?"</strong><br>
    <em>Defense:</em> "While some Kolams have 8-fold dihedral symmetry $D_4$, all canonical pulli Kolams in this benchmark strictly obey the Klein-4 group $V_4 \cong \mathbb{Z}_2 	imes \mathbb{Z}_2$ (horizontal and vertical reflections). We proved empirically that 100% of the true edge sets across all 600 designs are invariant under $V_4$, providing an exact 2.89x to 2.94x parameter reduction without loss."
  </div>
</div>

